From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 08:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: report what kills a thread or a process.

With the descriptor handover done, ntdll now reaches the server and dies on a
request instead:

    start_main_thread -> wine_server_call -> server_call_unlocked
                      -> abort_thread   (SIGSYS)

That is the branch a direct call takes when the request handler left the thread
without a reply pipe -- the server killed it while serving the request. Which
handler, and why, is not recorded anywhere: the thread simply stops having a
reply pipe, and every route from there ends in abort_thread, whose SIGSYS is
uncatchable and takes the title down.

Both ways a client loses its reply pipe now say so through klog, with the
address that asked for the kill and this function's own address to resolve it
against -- module bases move between runs, and a title that dies this way
leaves no usable crash dump of the server's own frames.
---
diff --git a/server/process.c b/server/process.c
index a0d95f4d38..49211ef01d 100644
--- a/server/process.c
+++ b/server/process.c
@@ -1031,6 +1031,19 @@ void resume_process( struct process *process )
 /* kill a process on the spot */
 void kill_process( struct process *process, int violent_death )
 {
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ DIAGNOSTIC -- see kill_thread. This is the other way a client's
+     * reply pipe disappears: a protocol error on the process socket. */
+    {
+        extern int sceKernelDebugOutText( int channel, const char *text );
+        char line[160];
+        snprintf( line, sizeof(line),
+                  "wineserver: kill_process %04x violent=%d from %p (anchor %p)\n",
+                  process->id, violent_death,
+                  __builtin_return_address(0), (void *)(uintptr_t)&kill_process );
+        sceKernelDebugOutText( 0, line );
+    }
+#endif
     if (!violent_death && process->msg_fd)  /* normal termination on pipe close */
     {
         release_object( process->msg_fd );
diff --git a/server/thread.c b/server/thread.c
index 32afbe9653..7395019835 100644
--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -1682,6 +1682,24 @@ int thread_get_inflight_fd( struct thread *thread, int client )
 void kill_thread( struct thread *thread, int violent_death )
 {
     if (thread->state == TERMINATED) return;  /* already killed */
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ DIAGNOSTIC. A killed thread loses its reply pipe, and every way the
+     * client then learns of it -- end of file on the pipe, or status 2 from a
+     * direct call -- ends in abort_thread, which raises the uncatchable SIGSYS
+     * and takes the title down with no indication of what decided to kill it.
+     * Name the caller. Module bases move, so print this function's own address
+     * too: base = (anchor) - (kill_thread's offset in the unstripped
+     * .shared.elf), which makes the return address resolvable. */
+    {
+        extern int sceKernelDebugOutText( int channel, const char *text );
+        char line[160];
+        snprintf( line, sizeof(line),
+                  "wineserver: kill_thread %04x exit=%d violent=%d from %p (anchor %p)\n",
+                  thread->id, thread->exit_code, violent_death,
+                  __builtin_return_address(0), (void *)(uintptr_t)&kill_thread );
+        sceKernelDebugOutText( 0, line );
+    }
+#endif
     thread->state = TERMINATED;
     thread->exit_time = current_time;
     if (current == thread) current = NULL;
